      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "At higher pH levels, the concentration of hydroxyl ions ($\\text{OH}^-$) in the soil solution increases significantly, causing a shift in the speciation of $\\text{Fe}^{2+}$ and $\\text{Mn}^{2+}$. These ions react with the abundant hydroxide ions to form insoluble precipitates, such as iron (III) hydroxide ($\\text{Fe}(\\text{OH})_3$) or manganese oxides. This reduction in soluble ionic forms effectively locks the micronutrients into inert solid phases within the soil, preventing them from being absorbed by root surfaces.",
      "source": "target",
